## (a) Primes representable as $x^2 + 16y^2$

**Claim:** A prime $p > 2$ can be written as $p = x^2 + 16y^2$ if and only if $p \equiv 1 \pmod{8}$.

**Proof.**

$(\Rightarrow)$ Suppose $p = x^2 + 16y^2$. Since $p$ is an odd prime, $x$ must be odd (if $x$ were even, then $x^2 + 16y^2$ would be even, contradicting $p > 2$ odd). For odd $x$, $x^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{8}$, and $16y^2 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}$. Therefore $p \equiv 1 \pmod{8}$.

$(\Leftarrow)$ Suppose $p \equiv 1 \pmod{8}$. Then $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$, so by Fermat's two-square theorem, $p = a^2 + b^2$ for some integers $a, b$. Since $p$ is odd, exactly one of $a, b$ is even and the other is odd. The odd one has square $\equiv 1 \pmod{8}$. Since $p \equiv 1 \pmod{8}$, the even one must have square $\equiv 0 \pmod{8}$. Writing the even one as $2k$, its square is $4k^2 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}$, which requires $k$ even, i.e., $4 \mid (2k)$. Setting $b = 4y$ (the even one) and $a = x$ (the odd one), we get $p = x^2 + 16y^2$. $\square$

